+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time to a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole
+number of seconds, like `120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups using the units `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m`
+(minutes), and `s` (seconds), like `2d`, `90m`, `1h30m`, or `1d2h3m4s`. Each unit may appear at most once, in that
+order, without spaces. Units are case-insensitive, so `45S` is the same as `45s`. Negative and decimal values are
+rejected. A {class}`datetime.timedelta` passed as the value, for example as the `default`, is used unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s", show_default=True)
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting for {timeout}.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=30m1h'])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.5.1
+```
+
